{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"f59e1138c428585cc4cde26ebc1126a77e13e29d740c0dc401009946935d62c3","slug":"pages.m05-causation-and-lurking-variables-5-of-5","location":"/pages/064-m05-causation-and-lurking-variables-5-of-5.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Causation and Lurking Variables (5 of 5)","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"064-m05-causation-and-lurking-variables-5-of-5.md","url":"/textbook/build/064-m05-causation-an-ad3b913ea610735331159a60241a77e2.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning Objectives","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"UwqvZVXcPa"}],"key":"FVwbEuOMvN"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recognize the distinction between association and causation, and identify potential lurking variables for explaining an observed relationship.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"yesTJLVn88"}],"key":"KMbx2Zphm6"}],"key":"RXdy8RPR9s"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recognize and explain the phenomenon of Simpson’s Paradox as it relates to interpreting the relationship between two variables.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"LePFRfUc6Z"}],"key":"tfoXCLODhP"}],"key":"CmwxV77242"}],"key":"wVxxFZKkch"}],"class":"note","key":"eOdyEAvZiY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"HeSGN52clA"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"not","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"CkePYlOU2r"}],"key":"rTQGJjo8CW"},{"type":"text","value":" always the case that including a lurking variable makes us rethink the direction of the association. In the next example we will see how including a lurking variable just helps us gain a deeper understanding of the observed relationship.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ieXN65nCoS"}],"key":"gWYf1nm3VU"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: College Entrance Exams","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"gfX9iNuiRr"}],"key":"sW5jdZuiZr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As discussed earlier, in the United States, the SAT is a widely used college entrance examination, required by the most prestigious schools. In some states, a different college entrance examination is prevalent, the ACT. A scatterplot of states’ average SAT scores against the percentage of students taking the SAT reveals two distinct clusters: states where most students take the SAT (with lower average scores) and states where few students take it (with higher average scores, since only a select group of students—often those applying to out-of-state schools—take the SAT there). Including the lurking variable “percentage of students taking the exam” doesn’t reverse any association, but it explains the clusters and deepens our understanding of the relationship.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"xesVSjHHJB"}],"key":"OwLhK7XepZ"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Video","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"KsXk7xhluw"}],"key":"bb5GikbGdA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nnj1YlqzkX4","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Including a Lurking Variable","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"PU07oRg11Q"}],"urlSource":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nnj1YlqzkX4","key":"meiPhjmFLd"}],"key":"prqOx9zSZg"}],"key":"RQMRUBvmE8"}],"class":"tip","key":"k3WwvsMjVO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The last two examples showed us that including a lurking variable in our exploration may","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"GYorDzzJXx"}],"key":"x8dg0sjPUp"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lead us to rethink the direction of an association (as in the Hospital/Death Rate example).","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"yC9tYeVodY"}],"key":"w58Kj71tsF"}],"key":"pJOn4nZjds"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Help us to gain a deeper understanding of the relationship between variables (as in the SAT/ACT example).","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"bFgzOguHov"}],"key":"uJGOWHWGnM"}],"key":"sJ2GulV2Ml"}],"key":"Wmeo0cecPG"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concept Check","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"YInyj4f3Ax"}],"identifier":"concept-check","label":"Concept Check","html_id":"concept-check","implicit":true,"key":"hJWl3hNuOc"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-abedf03535fddfc26081d8ab58b0b651.mjs","model":{"question":"A university finds that overall, program X admits a higher percentage of applicants than program Y. But when applicants are separated by intended major, program Y admits a higher percentage in every single major. What phenomenon is this?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Simpson's paradox","feedback":"Correct! When accounting for a lurking variable reverses the direction of an association, we have Simpson's paradox."},{"correct":false,"text":"Extrapolation","feedback":"Extrapolation refers to predicting beyond the range of the data in regression."},{"correct":false,"text":"Negative correlation","feedback":"A negative correlation describes two quantitative variables; this is about a reversal of an association after conditioning on a third variable."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Including the lurking variable (major) reversed the direction of the association.","explanation":""},"key":"PQIhSPssfC"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-abedf03535fddfc26081d8ab58b0b651.mjs","model":{"question":"In the hospital example, why did Hospital A have the higher overall death rate even though its death rate was lower within both severity groups?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Hospital A admitted a much larger proportion of severely ill patients, who have higher death rates everywhere","feedback":"Correct! Hospital A treated a far higher proportion of severely ill patients (1,500 of 2,100), and severely ill patients die at higher rates no matter where they are treated—this weighting drove up A's overall rate."},{"correct":false,"text":"The overall percentages were computed incorrectly","feedback":"The arithmetic is correct in both analyses; the difference comes from the patient mix, not an error."},{"correct":false,"text":"Hospital B's sample was too small to compute death rates","feedback":"Sample size alone doesn't explain the reversal—the key is the proportion of severe cases at each hospital."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Compare the mix of patients each hospital admitted.","explanation":""},"key":"Qw1DJRcjvd"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s Summarize","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"AKQVhbMpTN"}],"identifier":"lets-summarize","label":"Let’s Summarize","html_id":"lets-summarize","implicit":true,"key":"UY1iivtMj0"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A ","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"key":"A2JM9QxMQc"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"lurking variable","position":